{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788131950,"nonce":"Pfcqm6pQs2YDuWNsoXlCXqA7oik=","title":"Depends: 1.3 (advance loop, done), 2.2 + 2.4 (the fields a predicate reads).","message":"Wave 3. Split out of DESIGN.md's Time model on 2026-08-30, session 4.\n\n\nDESIGN.md advertised `advance until:{letter|alert|event-match|condition}` from\nspec stage. `TimeDriver` shipped `letter|threat|alert|event`. So `condition`\nnever existed and `threat` was never documented — found by 1.4's worker reading\nthe C# against the prose, not by any review.\n\nDESIGN has been corrected to describe what runs. `condition` is filed here\nrather than deleted, because it is not a naming mismatch — it is a real missing\ncapability, and the argument for it is DESIGN's own:\n\n**Every shipped matcher is a journal tap over discrete events.** It hooks\n`Journal.OnEvent` and halts on something that HAPPENED. Nothing is emitted when\na continuous value crosses a threshold, so no existing matcher can express\n\"stop when food gets low\" — only \"stop when the game finally complained\".\n\nAnd DESIGN says, in §Observation model, that **alerts fire LATE**: \"tattered-\napparel alert means the mood penalty already landed.\" `Alert_LowFood` is a\nlagging indicator. `food_days \u003c 3` is a leading one. A state-predicate matcher\nis the direct answer to a problem this project has already written down.\n\n\n`advance {until:{condition:{...}}, ...}` — halt when a predicate over observable\nstate becomes true. Evaluated on a cadence (NOT every tick; the digest's own\nsections are the natural sampling unit), on the main thread, read-only.\n\n- Predicate surface is the serializer field set, addressed by path:\n  `digest.resources.food_days`, `digest.power.gain_w`,\n  `digest.colonists[*].mood_pct`. Operators: `\u003c \u003c= \u003e \u003e= == !=`, plus `any`/`all`\n  over list-valued sections.\n- Report WHY it halted the same way the other matchers do: the path, the value\n  that tripped it, and the tick — not merely `reason: \"condition\"`.\n- Cadence is a knob with a sane default; a predicate is not free and the tick\n  loop's budget is the whole point of 1.3.\n- Composition with `timeout_ticks` and `halt_on_error` unchanged.\n\n\nObservers never mutate — a predicate evaluation must be exactly as read-only as\nthe digest it reads, including the lazy-getter discipline (2.6's exemplar\ncomments name the traps: `FreeColonistsSpawned` rebuilds on access,\n`DangerWatcher.DangerRating` re-enters it, `Room.Role` is a full lazy analysis).\nA predicate that is expensive per evaluation is a defect even if correct.\n\n\nPath syntax vs a small expression language; whether predicates may span sections;\nwhether a false-at-start predicate should halt immediately or require an edge\n(the second is probably right and is the difference between \"wait until\" and\n\"assert now\"); how 5.1 reuses this as an assertion primitive rather than\nreimplementing it.\n\n\n- `advance {until:{condition:{path:\"resources.food_days\", op:\"\u003c\", value:N}}}`\n  halts within one cadence window of the crossing, on a colony driven into it,\n  and reports the path and tripping value.\n- It halts EARLIER than the corresponding vanilla alert on the same run —\n  demonstrated with both in one advance, since being a leading indicator is the\n  entire justification for the feature.\n- A predicate that is never true exits on `timeout_ticks`, not by hanging.\n- Evaluation cost measured against the advance budget at the default cadence,\n  the way 1.2 measured journal overhead (0.0039 ms/frame through DPA).\n- Zero red errors; no mutation observable across an advance that only evaluates.","files":null}]}